window.HDH_SEARCH=[{"t":"Getting started","s":"","u":"docs/getting-started/","x":"Expenity keeps your money, bills and goals in one place, on every device you use. This guide takes about five minutes, and every step after the account can be skipped."},{"t":"Getting started","s":"1. Create an account (or sign in)","u":"docs/getting-started/#1-create-an-account-or-sign-in","x":"Open the app at expenity.github.io/expenity/app/. The first screen asks you to create an account or sign in. You use a passkey , which is your fingerprint, face or device PIN, so there is no password to remember or lose. Type your email, press Create account , and your device asks you to confirm. !The welcome screen. Expenity then shows ten recovery codes . Save them somewhere safe. They are how you get back in if you lose your passkeys, and they are shown only once. See Account and sync. Not ready for an account? Press Try without an account . Your data then stays in this browser only, and yo"},{"t":"Getting started","s":"2. Add Expenity to your home screen","u":"docs/getting-started/#2-add-expenity-to-your-home-screen","x":"The next screen shows, with a short animation, how to add Expenity to your home screen on iPhone, Android or a computer, so it opens like an app. Choose your device, follow the four steps, then press I added it , or Skip for now . More in Install on your device. !The install guide."},{"t":"Getting started","s":"3. Set it up","u":"docs/getting-started/#3-set-it-up","x":"A short setup asks a few questions. Each one can be skipped, and you can change every answer later. - Your name , used in the greeting. - Your main currency . Totals and reports use it. - What you want help with , such as tracking spending, saving for a trip, or cutting subscriptions. Expenity uses this to suggest things that fit you. See Budgets, goals and income. - A first goal , if you chose to save for something. - Your wallets : cash, bank account, card, savings, with what is in each. - How it looks : a theme and whether animations are on. See Themes and animations. !Choosing what you wan"},{"t":"Getting started","s":"4. Add your first expense","u":"docs/getting-started/#4-add-your-first-expense","x":"Press the green + button, choose Expense , type the amount on the keypad, add a description, pick a category and a wallet, and press the check mark. More about adding money. !The Home screen."},{"t":"Getting started","s":"Find your way around","u":"docs/getting-started/#find-your-way-around","x":"- Home : your total balance, income and expenses this month, ideas for you, wallets, what is due soon. - Activity : every transaction, by month, with search and filters. - Bills : what is coming, as a list or a calendar. - Reports : charts and tables. See Reports. - Plan (tablet and computer side bar, or from Home and More ): budgets, goals, income and ideas. - More : wallets, categories, reminders, backup, themes, your account."},{"t":"Getting started","s":"Where to go next","u":"docs/getting-started/#where-to-go-next","x":"- Add expenses, income and bills - Wallets and currencies - Budgets, goals and income - Shared wallets"},{"t":"Install on your device","s":"","u":"docs/install-on-your-device/","x":"Expenity is a web app. You can use it in a browser tab, or add it to your home screen so it opens full screen with its own icon, like any other app. The first-run flow shows this with a short animation; these are the same steps. !The install guide in the app."},{"t":"Install on your device","s":"iPhone and iPad","u":"docs/install-on-your-device/#iphone-and-ipad","x":"1. Open Expenity in Safari . 2. Tap the Share button. 3. Choose Add to Home Screen . 4. Tap Add , then open Expenity from your home screen."},{"t":"Install on your device","s":"Android","u":"docs/install-on-your-device/#android","x":"1. Open Expenity in Chrome . 2. Open the menu (⋮). 3. Choose Install app (or Add to Home screen ) and confirm. If your browser offers it, the install step in the app has an Install now button that does this for you."},{"t":"Install on your device","s":"Computer","u":"docs/install-on-your-device/#computer","x":"In Chrome or Edge, click the install icon at the right end of the address bar, or open the menu and choose Install Expenity . In Safari on a Mac: File → Add to Dock."},{"t":"Install on your device","s":"Offline","u":"docs/install-on-your-device/#offline","x":"After the first visit the app files are stored on your device, so Expenity opens without a connection. Changes you make offline are saved on the device and sync when you are back online. If a new version is available, it updates the next time you open the app with a connection."},{"t":"Install on your device","s":"Several devices","u":"docs/install-on-your-device/#several-devices","x":"Sign in with the same account on each device. Your wallets, transactions, bills, budgets, goals and settings follow you. How the look is set (theme, animations, language) is remembered per device. See Account and sync."},{"t":"Add expenses, income and bills","s":"","u":"docs/add-money/","x":"Everything starts with the green + button. It offers four things: an expense , an income , a transfer between wallets, and a bill ."},{"t":"Add expenses, income and bills","s":"Add an expense or income","u":"docs/add-money/#add-an-expense-or-income","x":"The sheet is built around the amount, so the most important thing comes first. 1. Choose Expense , Income or Transfer at the top. 2. Type the amount on the keypad. 3. Add a description if you like, for example \"Lunch\". 4. Pick a category . Common ones are shown; More opens the rest, including your own categories. 5. Pick the wallet . 6. Pick the date : Today, Yesterday, or another date. Note adds a longer note. 7. Press the check mark. !The add sheet with the keypad."},{"t":"Add expenses, income and bills","s":"Or type a sentence","u":"docs/add-money/#or-type-a-sentence","x":"Press Type it instead and write what happened, in English or Greek: - Coffee 3.50 yesterday - Salary 1500 income - Groceries 42,30 - Καφές 3,50 χθες Expenity fills the amount, description, date and type, and shows chips so you can see what it understood."},{"t":"Add expenses, income and bills","s":"Transfers","u":"docs/add-money/#transfers","x":"A transfer moves money between two of your wallets, for example from the bank to cash. It is not an expense. If the wallets use different currencies, Expenity asks how much arrived in the second one. A transfer works between two private wallets, or between two wallets in the same shared wallet, not across the two."},{"t":"Add expenses, income and bills","s":"Bills","u":"docs/add-money/#bills","x":"A bill is something that comes up again and again, like rent or Netflix. Press + and choose Bill . - Type it in a sentence : Netflix 15.99 monthly on the 3rd , Rent €850 every month on 1st , Electricity ~60 monthly 15th (a tilde means the amount changes each time). - Pick a template : Rent, Electricity, Water, Internet, Mobile phone, Netflix, Spotify, Car insurance, Credit card, Gym, Property tax, School fees. Greek templates such as ΔΕΗ, ΕΥΔΑΠ and ΕΝΦΙΑ are included. - Or fill in the form : name, the wallet it is paid from, amount, next due date, how often it repeats (once, weekly, every 2 we"},{"t":"Add expenses, income and bills","s":"Mark a bill paid","u":"docs/add-money/#mark-a-bill-paid","x":"On the Bills screen, Paid records the payment as an expense in the wallet and moves a repeating bill to its next date. You can Undo right after. For a bill with a variable amount, Expenity asks how much you paid. Other actions, from the ⋯ menu on a bill: Snooze (to tomorrow or for 3 days), Skip this time , open the payment link, copy the payment reference, edit and delete."},{"t":"Add expenses, income and bills","s":"Swipe and pull","u":"docs/add-money/#swipe-and-pull","x":"- On a transaction: swipe left to delete, right to edit. - On a bill: swipe right to mark it paid, left to delete. - Pull the screen down to sync now (when you are signed in). !The Activity screen."},{"t":"Add expenses, income and bills","s":"Edit and delete","u":"docs/add-money/#edit-and-delete","x":"Tap any row to see its details, edit it or delete it. On a tablet or computer the details show in a side panel. In a shared wallet, the details also say who added it, and a viewer can look but not change."},{"t":"Wallets and currencies","s":"","u":"docs/wallets-and-currencies/","x":"A wallet is a place where money is: cash, a bank account, a card, a savings account. Every transaction belongs to a wallet, and a wallet's balance is its opening balance plus income, minus expenses, with transfers in and out. !Your wallets."},{"t":"Wallets and currencies","s":"Add and edit wallets","u":"docs/wallets-and-currencies/#add-and-edit-wallets","x":"Open More → Wallets , or tap a wallet on Home. Each wallet has a name, a type (cash, bank account, card, savings, other), a currency and an opening balance , which is what was in it when you started. You can archive a wallet to hide it. A wallet that already has transactions is archived instead of deleted, so history is never lost."},{"t":"Wallets and currencies","s":"Your main currency","u":"docs/wallets-and-currencies/#your-main-currency","x":"You choose a main currency in the setup, and can change it in More → Reminders and calendar → Main currency . Totals on Home and in Reports are shown in it."},{"t":"Wallets and currencies","s":"Wallets in other currencies","u":"docs/wallets-and-currencies/#wallets-in-other-currencies","x":"A wallet can use any currency. A bill or transaction takes the currency of its wallet. When your wallets use several currencies, totals are converted to your main currency using daily exchange rates, and shown with ≈ to say they are approximate. Expenity never adds different currencies together as plain numbers. Rates are the European Central Bank reference rates, fetched through the Expenity API once a day. Only the public rates are requested: nothing about you is sent. If rates cannot be loaded, other currencies are listed on their own lines instead of being converted."},{"t":"Wallets and currencies","s":"Transfers","u":"docs/wallets-and-currencies/#transfers","x":"To move money between wallets, press + and choose Transfer . If the two wallets use different currencies, you also enter the amount received. Transfers are not counted as income or expenses."},{"t":"Wallets and currencies","s":"Money set aside for goals","u":"docs/wallets-and-currencies/#money-set-aside-for-goals","x":"When you add money to a goal, it is taken from a wallet you choose, so the wallet balance drops. That money is not counted as spending in your reports or budgets."},{"t":"Budgets, goals and income","s":"","u":"docs/budgets-goals-income/","x":"The Plan area has four tabs: Budgets , Goals , Income and Ideas . Open it from the side bar on a tablet or computer, from the Plan section on Home, or from More ."},{"t":"Budgets, goals and income","s":"Budgets","u":"docs/budgets-goals-income/#budgets","x":"A budget is a limit for a category (or for everything) over a period. 1. Open Plan → Budgets → Add budget . 2. Choose a category, or Everything . 3. Set the limit, and the period: monthly, weekly or yearly. 4. Optionally turn on Carry over what is left from the last period . !Budgets with progress bars. Each budget shows what you spent, what is left, a progress bar that turns amber at 80 percent and red at 100 percent, and an amount you can spend per day for the rest of the period."},{"t":"Budgets, goals and income","s":"Goals","u":"docs/budgets-goals-income/#goals","x":"A goal is something you save for: a trip, a car, a gift, an emergency fund. 1. Open Plan → Goals → Add goal . 2. Give it a name, a target amount, an optional deadline and an icon. 3. Press Add money on the goal to move money in from a wallet, or Take out to move it back. !Goals with progress rings. The ring shows progress. Expenity estimates the date you will reach the goal from how you have been saving over the last 90 days, and, when you set a deadline, how much per month would get you there. You get a small celebration at 25, 50, 75 and 100 percent. The Goals tab also shows a savings overvi"},{"t":"Budgets, goals and income","s":"Recurring income","u":"docs/budgets-goals-income/#recurring-income","x":"Add your salary or other regular income as a schedule: a name, the wallet it arrives in, the amount, the next date and how often. When it arrives, press Received . Expenity records the income in the wallet and moves the schedule to the next date (you can undo). Expected income feeds the forecast."},{"t":"Budgets, goals and income","s":"Ideas for you","u":"docs/budgets-goals-income/#ideas-for-you","x":"In the setup you can say what you want from Expenity, and you can change it any time in Plan → Ideas: track spending, save for something, build savings, cut subscriptions, spend less on everyday things, never miss a bill, pay off debt. Expenity then looks at your own data, on your device, and offers specific ideas, for example: - A subscription you could cancel, with what it costs a year and how much of a goal that would cover. - Delivery or eating out over the last 30 days, and what cooking at home half of the time would save. Expenity recognises these from the names you type, such as efood, "},{"t":"Reports","s":"","u":"docs/reports/","x":"Reports turn your transactions into charts and tables. The All view shows every report one after the other, each with an Open link, so you can scroll through everything. The buttons under the filters (each with an icon) open one report on its own. Pick a period at the top (This month, Last month, 3 months, This year, 12 months, or a custom range), then a tab. Filters narrow everything to a wallet, a category, only expenses or only income, or a word in the description. Goal money is left out of spending. !The Overview tab."},{"t":"Reports","s":"Overview","u":"docs/reports/#overview","x":"Net worth, net result, income, expenses, savings rate and spending per day, each with an arrow and a percentage compared with the previous period of the same length. Below, the top categories and your biggest expense."},{"t":"Reports","s":"Spending","u":"docs/reports/#spending","x":"A donut chart by category. Tap a slice or a legend row to drill down into that category's transactions. Also: average spending by weekday, where the money goes (by description) and the biggest expenses. !Spending by category."},{"t":"Reports","s":"Cash flow","u":"docs/reports/#cash-flow","x":"Income and expenses month by month, cumulative cash flow, and your savings rate by month."},{"t":"Reports","s":"Trends","u":"docs/reports/#trends","x":"Spending by month stacked by category (the top five and the rest), and how this month compares with last month."},{"t":"Reports","s":"Forecast","u":"docs/reports/#forecast","x":"The expected balance in 30, 60 and 90 days, drawn as a line. It uses your bills, your recurring income and a typical daily spend worked out from the last 60 days, and says so under the chart. It also shows the amount you can spend per day until the end of the month, the lowest point your balance reaches and the biggest payments coming up. !The forecast."},{"t":"Reports","s":"Subscriptions","u":"docs/reports/#subscriptions","x":"A table of your subscriptions with the monthly and yearly cost and the next charge, the total per year, and a note when a price went up."},{"t":"Reports","s":"Table","u":"docs/reports/#table","x":"Every transaction in the period, in a table you can sort by clicking a heading and group by category, wallet or month, with totals. CSV downloads it for a spreadsheet. Every chart has Show as a table , so the numbers are always available as text. On a tablet or computer the charts and tiles use the extra room. !Reports on a tablet."},{"t":"Reminders","s":"","u":"docs/reminders/","x":"Expenity can remind you in three ways. The first one works like a normal app: a notification arrives even when Expenity is closed."},{"t":"Reminders","s":"1. Notifications when the app is closed","u":"docs/reminders/#1-notifications-when-the-app-is-closed","x":"With an account, Expenity sends you one summary a day at the hour you choose, listing the bills that are due soon or overdue. It arrives like any app notification, on the lock screen, even if Expenity is not open. 1. Sign in (an account is needed, because Expenity has to know which bills are due). 2. Open More → Reminders and calendar → Notifications on this device and press Turn on . Allow notifications when your browser asks. 3. Choose the time you want it. Send a test shows you what it looks like. Things to know: - iPhone and iPad: Apple only allows this for apps added to the Home Screen. A"},{"t":"Reminders","s":"2. Your calendar (works without an account)","u":"docs/reminders/#2-your-calendar-works-without-an-account","x":"This is the reminder to rely on. 1. Open More → Reminders and calendar . 2. Press Download calendar file . 3. Import the file into your calendar app. The file holds every open bill (not recurring income or budgets) as an all-day event, with its repeat rule and two alarms: at 09:00 on the reminder day you chose, and at 09:00 on the due day. Your calendar app then does the reminding on every device where that calendar syncs. How to import: - Google Calendar (on a computer): Settings → Import & export → Import, choose the file. - Apple Calendar : open the file, or File → Import, and choose a cale"},{"t":"Reminders","s":"3. Notifications while Expenity is open","u":"docs/reminders/#3-notifications-while-expenity-is-open","x":"In More → Reminders and calendar → Notifications while Expenity is open , press Turn on and allow notifications when your browser asks. When you open Expenity or keep it in a tab, you get one notification a day for bills that are due within their reminder window and are not snoozed. This does not work when the app is fully closed. For that, use notifications when the app is closed (above) or the calendar file."},{"t":"Reminders","s":"Choose how early","u":"docs/reminders/#choose-how-early","x":"In More → Reminders and calendar choose the Default reminder , from the day itself up to two weeks before. Each bill can override it in the Remind me field."},{"t":"Reminders","s":"Snooze","u":"docs/reminders/#snooze","x":"In a bill's ⋯ menu, Snooze to tomorrow or Snooze 3 days stops its notifications for a while."},{"t":"Reminders","s":"What is coming","u":"docs/reminders/#what-is-coming","x":"Email reminders need a sending domain and are planned. Notifications on the lock screen and the calendar file cover the same need today."},{"t":"Shared wallets","s":"","u":"docs/shared-wallets/","x":"A shared wallet lets family or friends see and add to the same wallet from their own devices, for a household budget, a trip or a shared account. Only the wallet you share is visible to others: your other wallets, budgets and goals stay private. !The sharing sheet."},{"t":"Shared wallets","s":"Share a wallet","u":"docs/shared-wallets/#share-a-wallet","x":"You need an account (not guest mode). 1. Open More → Wallets and tap the wallet. 2. Press Share this wallet , then Start sharing . 3. Choose a role for the person you invite: Can edit or Can view . 4. Press Create invite . You get a code and a link. Send either one to the person, in a message or in person. An invite works once and expires after 7 days. You can see open invites and cancel them. You can share up to 5 wallets, with up to 12 people each."},{"t":"Shared wallets","s":"Join a shared wallet","u":"docs/shared-wallets/#join-a-shared-wallet","x":"Open the link you were sent, or go to More → Wallets → Join a shared wallet and type the code. You need an account, and Expenity asks you to sign in or create one first if you do not have one. The wallet and its history then appear on your device."},{"t":"Shared wallets","s":"Roles","u":"docs/shared-wallets/#roles","x":"Role Can do Owner Everything: invite people, change roles, remove people, stop sharing Can edit See everything in the wallet, add and change transactions and bills Can view See everything in the wallet, but not change it The server checks the role on every change, not only the app. Each transaction and bill shows who added it."},{"t":"Shared wallets","s":"What is shared","u":"docs/shared-wallets/#what-is-shared","x":"The wallet, its transactions and its bills. Budgets, goals, recurring income and your settings are not shared. Transfers only work between two private wallets or between two wallets inside the same shared wallet."},{"t":"Shared wallets","s":"Privacy","u":"docs/shared-wallets/#privacy","x":"People in a shared wallet can see each other's email address and everything in that wallet. Tell the people you invite what they will see."},{"t":"Shared wallets","s":"Leave, remove, stop sharing","u":"docs/shared-wallets/#leave-remove-stop-sharing","x":"- A member can Leave this wallet . It is removed from their device and the others keep it. - The owner can Remove someone, who loses access and the data on their device. - The owner can Stop sharing and keep a copy : everyone else loses access, and you keep the wallet and all its transactions as a private wallet. If you delete your account, wallets you own and share are deleted for everyone."},{"t":"Themes and animations","s":"","u":"docs/themes-and-look/","x":"Expenity has twelve themes. A theme changes more than colour: shapes, type, borders and a few details too. Pick one in More → Appearance and language , or during setup. !Six of the themes. Theme Look Emerald The default: fresh green, soft cards Minimal Black, white and hairlines Paper notes Ruled paper, handwriting, a sticky note for your balance Ledger An accounting book, serif type, double rules Retro terminal Green on black, monospace, scanlines Synthwave Neon pink and cyan with a grid horizon Blueprint White lines on blue drawing paper Pastel candy Soft, round and playful Midnight True bla"},{"t":"Themes and animations","s":"Animations","u":"docs/themes-and-look/#animations","x":"Expenity moves a little: screens slide in, charts grow, buttons respond when pressed. Under Animations you can choose: - Match device : follows your device's \"reduce motion\" setting. - Full : all animations. - Reduced : a very short version of each. - Off : no animations at all. Theme, light or dark and animations are remembered on each device. Language is too: switch it any time in More → Appearance and language, or from the welcome screen. !Dark mode."},{"t":"Account and sync","s":"","u":"docs/account-and-sync/","x":"An account lets you use Expenity on all your devices and keeps your data safe if you lose one. It is free."},{"t":"Account and sync","s":"Passkeys, not passwords","u":"docs/account-and-sync/#passkeys-not-passwords","x":"You sign in with a passkey : your fingerprint, face or device PIN. Your device creates it, and Expenity never sees a password because there is none to forget, reuse or steal. Many devices sync passkeys between your own devices (iCloud Keychain, Google Password Manager, a password manager). - Create an account : type your email and confirm on your device. - Sign in : press Sign in with a passkey and choose your account."},{"t":"Account and sync","s":"Recovery codes","u":"docs/account-and-sync/#recovery-codes","x":"When you create an account you get ten one-time recovery codes . If you lose all your passkeys, enter your email and one code ( Lost your passkey? ) to get back in. You then add a new passkey, and every other device is signed out. Each code works once and they cannot be shown again, so keep them somewhere safe: a password manager or a printout. You can make a new set in More → Account and sync ."},{"t":"Account and sync","s":"Your devices","u":"docs/account-and-sync/#your-devices","x":"More → Account and sync lists your passkeys and the devices that are signed in. You can add another passkey, remove one (you always keep at least one) and sign a device out."},{"t":"Account and sync","s":"How sync works","u":"docs/account-and-sync/#how-sync-works","x":"Expenity saves on your device first and syncs in the background, so it works offline. When you are back online, changes go up and other devices' changes come down. If two devices change the same item, the newest change wins for that item. Pull down on a screen to sync right now. The sync dot next to your avatar shows the state: green is synced. What syncs: wallets, transactions, bills, categories, budgets, goals, recurring income and some settings. Look settings (theme, animations, language) stay per device."},{"t":"Account and sync","s":"Sign out","u":"docs/account-and-sync/#sign-out","x":"Sign out removes the data from that device (it stays in your account). If something has not synced yet, Expenity tells you first."},{"t":"Account and sync","s":"Delete your account","u":"docs/account-and-sync/#delete-your-account","x":"More → Account and sync → Delete my account deletes your account and all your synced data from the server. It asks you to confirm with your passkey. It cannot be undone. Wallets you own and share are deleted for everyone."},{"t":"Account and sync","s":"Email","u":"docs/account-and-sync/#email","x":"Expenity stores your email to identify your account. Email verification and email reminders are not switched on yet: they need a sending domain, which is planned."},{"t":"Privacy and your data","s":"","u":"docs/privacy-and-your-data/","x":""},{"t":"Privacy and your data","s":"What is stored, and where","u":"docs/privacy-and-your-data/#what-is-stored-and-where","x":"On your device. Expenity saves everything in your browser first, so it works offline. Without an account, that is the only place your data lives. On Expenity's server, if you create an account. To sync between your devices, your email address and your data (wallets, transactions, bills, budgets, goals, categories, recurring income and some settings) are stored on a Cloudflare Worker and database (D1). The connection is encrypted, and access needs your passkey. Expenity does not look at your data, sell it, share it or use it for advertising. As with most online services, it is not end-to-end en"},{"t":"Privacy and your data","s":"Back up and restore","u":"docs/privacy-and-your-data/#back-up-and-restore","x":"Even with an account, you can keep your own copy. In More → Data and backup : - Download backup (JSON) saves your wallets, transactions, bills, budgets, goals and income schedules. - Restore a backup reads such a file and replaces the current data after you confirm. This is also how you move data from the guest mode of one browser to another. - Transactions as CSV exports your history for a spreadsheet. Without an account, clearing the site's data (or losing the device) removes your data, so back up regularly."},{"t":"Privacy and your data","s":"Delete everything","u":"docs/privacy-and-your-data/#delete-everything","x":"- Delete all my data (More → Data and backup) removes everything from this browser. If you are signed in, your data comes back from your account at the next sync, so it only clears this device. - Delete my account (More → Account and sync) deletes your account and everything stored on the server. It asks for your passkey and cannot be undone. Wallets you own and share are deleted for everyone."},{"t":"Privacy and your data","s":"Notifications while the app is open","u":"docs/privacy-and-your-data/#notifications-while-the-app-is-open","x":"The in-app option shows them locally. Nothing is sent anywhere."},{"t":"Privacy and your data","s":"The calendar file","u":"docs/privacy-and-your-data/#the-calendar-file","x":"The calendar file you download contains your bill names, amounts and notes. When you import it into Google, Apple or Outlook calendar, it is stored by that service under its own privacy rules. Avoid sensitive details in notes if that matters to you."},{"t":"Privacy and your data","s":"Open source","u":"docs/privacy-and-your-data/#open-source","x":"The app and the server are public, so you can check all of this: github.com/expenity/expenity."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"","u":"docs/faq/","x":""},{"t":"FAQ","s":"Is Expenity free?","u":"docs/faq/#is-expenity-free","x":"Yes, and it is open source. There are no ads and no paid tiers today."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"Does it work on my iPhone, Android phone, tablet and computer?","u":"docs/faq/#does-it-work-on-my-iphone-android-phone-tablet-and-computer","x":"Yes. It is a web app that runs in any modern browser, and you can add it to your home screen like an app: see Install on your device. Sign in with the same account on each device and your data follows you."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"Do I need an account?","u":"docs/faq/#do-i-need-an-account","x":"You can try Expenity without one, and your data then stays in that browser only. An account (created with a passkey, no password) gives you sync between devices, a backup in case you lose a device, and shared wallets. See Account and sync."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"What is a passkey?","u":"docs/faq/#what-is-a-passkey","x":"Your fingerprint, face or device PIN, used instead of a password. Your device keeps the secret; Expenity only stores a public key. If you lose your passkeys, the recovery codes you saved at sign-up get you back in."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"I lost my passkey and my recovery codes.","u":"docs/faq/#i-lost-my-passkey-and-my-recovery-codes","x":"Email verification is not switched on yet, so there is no other way in. This is the trade-off of having no passwords. Save your recovery codes when you create the account, and add a second passkey on another device in More → Account and sync."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"Will it remind me when the app is closed?","u":"docs/faq/#will-it-remind-me-when-the-app-is-closed","x":"Yes. With an account, turn on notifications in More → Reminders and calendar and Expenity sends one summary a day, at the hour you choose, even when the app is closed. On iPhone and iPad it needs the app added to the Home Screen. Without an account, download the calendar file and import it into your calendar. See Reminders."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"Why not just connect to my bank?","u":"docs/faq/#why-not-just-connect-to-my-bank","x":"Reading a bank account would make Expenity handle bank credentials and sensitive data, which we would rather avoid. You tell it what you spend and earn; it tells you where it goes."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"Where is my data stored? Can you read it?","u":"docs/faq/#where-is-my-data-stored-can-you-read-it","x":"On your device, and, if you have an account, on Expenity's server (Cloudflare) so it can sync. The connection is encrypted and only your passkey opens your account. Expenity does not look at your data, sell it or share it. See Privacy and your data."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"Can several people share a wallet?","u":"docs/faq/#can-several-people-share-a-wallet","x":"Yes. Share a wallet with an invite code or link and choose whether people can edit or only view. See Shared wallets."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"Does it support Greek?","u":"docs/faq/#does-it-support-greek","x":"Yes. Choose the language on the welcome screen, or in More → Appearance and language. The app, this help center and the website are in English and Greek, and the sentence box understands both. The Greek text is still being reviewed by a native speaker: please tell us what to improve."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"Which currencies?","u":"docs/faq/#which-currencies","x":"Any currency by its three-letter code. A wallet has one currency, and totals are converted to your main currency with daily European Central Bank rates, marked with ≈. See Wallets and currencies."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"Is the advice in \"Ideas\" financial advice?","u":"docs/faq/#is-the-advice-in-ideas-financial-advice","x":"No. Ideas are simple observations about your own data, such as \"this subscription costs €192 a year\". Decide for yourself."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"The sentence box got something wrong.","u":"docs/faq/#the-sentence-box-got-something-wrong","x":"It is a helper. Correct the fields below it. If you have a sentence that should work, please open an issue with the exact text."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"How do I report a bug or suggest something?","u":"docs/faq/#how-do-i-report-a-bug-or-suggest-something","x":"On GitHub."}];